t in the polling average toward single digits, or signs that Cassidy's non-endorsement splits Republican turnout in the runoff-style general, would warrant moving this toward a Lean R race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Bill Cassidy (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Louisiana's Senate seat is open after Bill Cassidy declined to seek another term, and Republican Julia Letlow enters the general election as a clear favorite in a state that leans about eleven points Republican. Letlow won the GOP nomination with Donald Trump's backing and has raised over $6 million, supplemented by heavy outside spending on both sides. Democrat Jamie Davis has raised about $2 million and faces steep structural odds.\n\nRecent Democratic-sponsored polls show a closer race than the state's fundamentals suggest, but prediction markets give Davis only a 5 to 9 percent chance of winning, and most expert raters call the race Solid or Safe Republican. Cassidy's decision not to endorse Letlow is a minor wrinkle, but the seat remains very likely to stay in Republican hands.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0491, "margin": -9.91}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0664, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0312, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0328, "margin": -12.0}]}